<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Ruby</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-ruby</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over Bundler (Gemfile beside this file). The commands below run ONLY
        under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for
        why opt-in is load-bearing. An ordinary `bin/build.sh -am` therefore builds this skeleton
        and runs no Ruby toolchain at all.

        THE TEST PHASE RUNS `rake`, NOT `rspec`, and the difference is deliberate: rake's default
        task is rubocop-then-spec, so the bug finder runs in the same lane as the specs and on a
        developer's box rather than only in CI. The clients matrix runs its own rubocop step on
        top; that is a redundancy, not the first place the check fires.

        THE SPECS NEED THE PROXY MODULE'S TEST JAR, AND THE PROFILE BELOW IS HOW MAVEN ORDERS IT.
        The conformance spec spawns the harness (Main, in parallel-consumer-proxy's own
        jar). An earlier note in this place said Maven could not order that for us and told the
        reader to build the proxy by hand first; it can, the same way the Go, Python and TypeScript
        modules already do it, and the clients matrix row - which cannot run a hand command - was
        red on exactly this. Locally the hand command still works and is still the fastest way:

            bin/build.sh -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy -am -DskipTests

        The spec fails with that command in its message rather than skipping, because a test that
        quietly does not run is not a passing test. Recorded as a wave finding in
        docs/inflight/clients/ruby.md (astubbs#242).
    -->
    <!--
        THE COMPILE PHASE RUNS scripts/build.sh, NOT `bundle install`, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
        Installing gems is not compiling: with `bundle install` as the whole of the compile phase,
        `mvn compile -Dpc.foreignClients` reported BUILD SUCCESS on a lib/ file ending in
        `this is not valid ruby @@@`, because nothing in the phase ever parsed a source file. The
        script installs the gems and then runs `rake syntax`, which is `ruby -c` over every source
        file; the script's header says why the two steps cannot be one property, and the Rakefile
        task says why `ruby -c` rather than RuboCop. Ruby wave of the language-proxy plan,
        astubbs#242.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>bash</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool ruby -- ./scripts/build.sh</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>bash</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>${pc.foreign.step} --tool ruby -- bundle exec rake</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            The sidecar this module's handshake test spawns. The clients aggregator owns the wiring
            and the reasoning - its `sidecar-classpath` execution, and why hoisting it is safe; what
            is here is the one thing that cannot be hoisted, the dependency that makes `-am` build
            parallel-consumer-proxy before this module's suite tries to spawn it. Test-scoped and
            inside the opt-in profile, so an ordinary build has no edge to the sidecar at all.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>ruby-sidecar-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <!--
        NO maven-clean-plugin FILESETS HERE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.

        Ruby compiles to nothing, and this module emits nothing of its own for `mvn clean` to
        remove. Measured against a tree carrying none of it: `bundle install` produced vendor/
        and nothing else, and `bundle exec rubocop` and `bundle exec rspec` produced nothing at
        all. A `git status` run afterwards, with the ignored files included, listed exactly one
        entry: vendor/. (An XML comment may not contain two consecutive dashes, which is why that
        command is described rather than quoted; it has broken a pom in this tree before.)

        Each of the four candidates, and why it is not a clean target:

        - vendor/bundle is the INSTALLED BUNDLE. That makes it this language's local repository,
          not build output; `mvn clean` deletes target/ and does not empty ~/.m2. Deleting it would
          cost a full reinstall of 27 gems, two of them with native extensions.
        - lib/parallelconsumer/ is protoc's output but it is COMMITTED (see .gitignore, which says
          why: a consumer of this library must find the stubs already there). Committed generated
          code is source here, and clean does not delete source.
        - RuboCop's cache is ~/.cache/rubocop_cache - outside the module, shared with every other
          checkout on the box, and out of bounds for that reason alone.
        - target/proxy-test-classpath.txt, written by the profile above, is under target/, which
          the default clean execution already removes. Everything else the specs write goes to
          Dir.mktmpdir, under the system temp directory.

        So the correct configuration for this module is none, and the answer is recorded here
        rather than left as an absence, because an empty configuration and an unconsidered one
        look identical.
    -->
    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
